```

Running Batfish requires several setup steps (to be honest, we also spent quite some time on this).  
Please make sure to start the Batfish Docker container before running the tests. Once set up, you can first try running the original notebooks from  
`https://github.com/batfish/batfish` and use any timing method of your choice. This should produce the same results as our provided notebooks.  
If anything still doesn’t work on your side, please let us know — we’ll be happy to help fix it.

As for the differences in output, this is expected. Our Relational NetKAT tool encodes each query as an equivalence judgment of the form `e` is empty or `e1 = e2`,  
so it only returns a boolean result (`true` or `false`).  
Batfish, on the other hand, is an industrial-strength tool capable of showing example traces in addition to boolean reachability outcomes.  
This is a direction we hope to explore in the future, but for now it can be ignored.  
In Batfish, a query is considered `true` when at least one trace is returned; otherwise, it is `false`.  
When using `actions="FAILURE"`, you can view it as checking for the complement of the reachability condition.


